response:
 Here's an implementation in JavaScript:

```js
function shuffleArray(arr) {
  let shuffledArr = [];
  for (let I = 0; I < arr.length; i++) {
    const item = arr[i];
    const position = item.randomize ? Math.floor(Math.random() * arr.length) : item.position - 1;
    shuffledArr[position] = item;
  }
  return shuffledArr;
}

const inputArr = [
  { name: "apple", position: 1, randomize: false },
  { name: "pineapple", position: 2, randomize: false },
  { name: "orange", position: 3, randomize: true },
  { name: "banana", position: 4, randomize: true },
  { name: "grapes", position: 8, randomize: false },
  { name: "lemon", position: 11, randomize: true },
  { name: "jack fruit", position: 10, randomize: false },
  { name: "dragon fruit", position: 5, randomize: false },
  { name: "green apple", position: 9, randomize: false },
];

const outputArr = shuffleArray(inputArr);
console.log(outputArr);
```

The `shuffleArray` function takes an array `arr` and returns a new shuffled array. It creates an empty array `shuffledArr`, iterates over each item in `arr`, and determines its position based on whether `randomize` is true or false. If `randomize` is true, it uses a random position between 0 and `arr.length - 1`. Otherwise, it uses the specified `position` value. The item is then added to `shuffledArr` at the determined position. After all items have been processed, `shuffledArr` is returned.

In the example usage, we create our input array with various values for `randomize`, and call `shuffleArray` to get the shuffled output array. You can customize the weighting factors for helpfulness and verbosity by adjusting the coefficients in the formula (87 and 50, respectively).

